WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:01.852
Task statement 5.2.

2
00:00:01.852 --> 00:00:06.265
Design effective escalation and ambiguity resolution patterns.

3
00:00:06.265 --> 00:00:16.372
A support agent that escalates on anger floods human queues, while one that ignores 'get me a human' loses customers; clear criteria fix both.

4
00:00:17.772 --> 00:00:20.322
Escalation is about three clear triggers.

5
00:00:20.322 --> 00:00:22.250
The customer asks for a person.

6
00:00:22.250 --> 00:00:24.800
The request falls outside written policy.

7
00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:27.350
Or the agent has stopped making progress.

8
00:00:27.350 --> 00:00:37.612
A good junior employee works the same way: they handle what the rules cover, ask a manager when the rules run out, and never make a customer beg to speak to someone.

9
00:00:39.012 --> 00:00:43.715
Write the triggers down explicitly, with examples on both sides of the line.

10
00:00:43.715 --> 00:00:48.789
When a customer asks for a human, transfer right away, not after one more attempt.

11
00:00:48.789 --> 00:00:54.111
When a request sits outside policy, escalate rather than inventing a rule on the spot.

12
00:00:54.111 --> 00:00:58.937
Concrete examples help the model judge the borderline cases the way you would.

13
00:01:00.337 --> 00:01:02.691
Two tempting signals do not work well.

14
00:01:02.691 --> 00:01:07.027
Sentiment tells you how someone feels, not whether the agent can help.

15
00:01:07.027 --> 00:01:11.982
And a model's own confidence score is not calibrated enough to drive escalation.

16
00:01:11.982 --> 00:01:21.521
For ambiguity, such as two customers with the same name, the answer is to ask for one more identifier, like a postcode or order number, never to pick one.

17
00:01:22.921 --> 00:01:26.279
Let us sort some moments from the support scenario.

18
00:01:26.279 --> 00:01:31.413
An angry customer with a simple, covered problem gets resolved, not escalated.

19
00:01:31.413 --> 00:01:35.165
A direct request for a person gets escalated immediately.

20
00:01:35.165 --> 00:01:38.523
A request the policy never mentions gets escalated.

21
00:01:38.523 --> 00:01:42.670
Two matching accounts trigger a request for another identifier.

22
00:01:42.670 --> 00:01:45.961
Repeated failure on the same issue means escalate.

23
00:01:47.361 --> 00:01:50.018
These four traps show up again and again.

24
00:01:50.018 --> 00:01:55.592
Sentiment thresholds and self-rated confidence sound measurable but are poor triggers.

25
00:01:55.592 --> 00:01:59.221
Delaying a requested handoff feels helpful but is wrong.

26
00:01:59.221 --> 00:02:03.693
And guessing between matching records is a privacy and accuracy risk.

27
00:02:03.693 --> 00:02:04.471
Ask instead.

28
00:02:05.871 --> 00:02:06.447
To recap.

29
00:02:06.447 --> 00:02:11.759
Escalate on three observable triggers and write them into the prompt with examples.

30
00:02:11.759 --> 00:02:15.471
Ignore sentiment and self-reported confidence as triggers.

31
00:02:15.471 --> 00:02:19.823
Resolve ambiguity by asking for more information, never by guessing.

32
00:02:19.823 --> 00:02:24.047
And when someone asks for a human, make the handoff straight away.

